## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a simple, slightly complex nutrition fact or slogan (e.g., "Antioxidants in fruits and vegetables improve cell health and boost metabolism.") on the board. Students are asked, "How would you explain this simply to a younger student or someone who doesn't speak much English?" This immediately primes students for mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to simplify and rephrase the nutrition fact. For example, "Antioxidants are like tiny superheroes in fruits and vegetables. If you eat them, they protect your body and give you energy." The teacher also models the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "antioxidant" and "metabolism," emphasizing stress, and uses "should" for advice (e.g., "You should eat more superfoods.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'mediate information,' does it mean to make it more difficult or easier to understand?" (Expected: Easier)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When would you need to explain something using simple words or two languages?" (Expected: When someone doesn't understand, for new students, for younger kids).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nutrition")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short school announcement text (approx. 70-80 words, A2.4 level) about a new "Healthy Choices Week" campaign, focusing on the benefits of a balanced diet and hydration. The text will include phrases like: "If you want to improve your health, you should eat a balanced diet with plenty of fruits, vegetables, and whole grains. Good nutrition is important because it gives you energy and helps your metabolism. Remember to stay hydrated!" It also includes some target vocabulary: <i>benefits, balanced diet, vitamins, minerals, hydration, metabolism.</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students demonstrate understanding of an authentic short notice related to school health initiatives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea of this announcement?" (Expected: About healthy eating choices at school).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read sentences based on the announcement and mark them True or False. E.g., "The announcement says vegetables give you energy." (True) or "Drinking water is not mentioned." (False).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match key vocabulary (e.g., 'balanced diet', 'hydration', 'metabolism') from the announcement to simple definitions provided.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice mediating information using the modal "should" for advice, first conditional for facts, and adverbial clauses for justification. They will use core vocabulary such as <i>nutrition, health, benefits, balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, grains, vitamins, minerals, nutrients, fiber, protein, carbohydrates, hydration, metabolism, superfoods.</i> Practice pronunciation of stress in words like "carbohydrate" and minimal pairs like /ɪ/ in "bit" vs /iː/ in "beat" when explaining quantities or types of food (e.g., "a little bit of sugar" vs "eat a whole beet").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Rephrasing:</b> The teacher writes sentences related to healthy eating using target grammar and vocabulary. In pairs, students rephrase each sentence to make it simpler, as if explaining to a new or younger student. They should practice using two languages if a term is difficult.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You should consume whole grains because they have fiber." (Rephrased: "Eat whole grains. They have fiber, which helps your digestion.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you eat enough protein, it will help your muscles." (Rephrased: "Protein helps your muscles. If you eat protein, your muscles will be strong.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher cues for pronunciation: "Remember to stress 'protein' and 'carbohydrates'."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Explanation Chain:</b> Students sit in small groups. The teacher provides a list of key nutrition terms (e.g., <i>balanced diet, nutrients, hydration, metabolism, superfoods</i>). One student starts by defining/explaining one term in simple English. The next student rephrases or adds to the explanation, using "because" or "if...then," ensuring everyone understands. If a term is particularly challenging, the teacher encourages them to use a familiar language to clarify, then try to explain it in English again. (e.g., "What is a 'balanced diet'? If you eat different types of food, like fruits and vegetables, you will have a balanced diet. It's important because it gives you all the nutrients. <i>'Nutrientes' son las cosas buenas que tu cuerpo necesita.</i>").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Advice Exchange:</b> Students work in pairs. One student gives a piece of dietary advice related to their "Nutrition Infographic" project (e.g., "You should eat more fruits and vegetables for a balanced diet."). The other student acts as a "new student" and asks clarifying questions, prompting the first student to mediate (explain, simplify, give examples, possibly use a familiar language for a word like "fiber" or "antioxidants"). They switch roles. (e.g., "What exactly is 'fiber'? If you eat fiber, it helps your digestion. It's like a broom for your body.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-17 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nutrition")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will mediate their "Nutrition Infographic" project information. Each student (or pair/group, depending on project structure) will present their infographic to a small group of peers (acting as "new students" or visitors). The primary task is to clearly and simply explain the concepts on their infographic about healthy food for a balanced diet. They must use "should" for advice, first conditional for health benefits (e.g., "If you eat well, you stay healthy"), and adverbial clauses for justification (e.g., "Good nutrition is important because it...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">During their presentation, students are expected to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explain key terms and benefits from their infographic (e.g., <i>balanced diet, nutrients, vitamins, minerals, hydration, protein, carbohydrates</i>) using simple, accessible language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Facilitate a short discussion, encouraging their "audience" to ask questions or share their own favorite healthy foods, ensuring everyone participates. They should use phrases like, "What do you think?", "Does anyone have a question about this?", "Who wants to share a healthy snack idea?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Be prepared to clarify information or translate key words into a familiar language if a peer expresses confusion (e.g., "What does 'fiber' mean? It's 'fibra,' it helps your digestion!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Focus on clear pronunciation, especially of multisyllabic words like "nutrition" and "carbohydrate."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, listening for mediation strategies, appropriate language use, and interaction facilitation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will mediate their Nutrition Infographic to a small group of classmates (acting as "new students"). The teacher observes and uses an observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Simplification:</b> Explains complex information using simple, understandable words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Language Use:</b> Accurately uses "should" for advice, first conditional, and "because" clauses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates a range of relevant nutrition vocabulary (e.g., <i>balanced diet, nutrients, hydration, superfoods</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Overall intelligible, with attention to stress in multisyllabic words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interaction Facilitation:</b> Guides discussion, encourages participation, and responds to peer questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of two languages (if needed):</b> Effectively clarifies terms by using a familiar language when English is insufficient.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one piece of information from your infographic you mediated well, and how did you make it simple?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you use two languages to help someone understand? How did that help?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn about explaining complex topics like nutrition to others today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When might you need to mediate information about health or other topics in your daily life?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What mediation strategies worked best for students? Was the balance between English and familiar language appropriate? Were the A2.4 level tasks adequately challenging yet achievable? What adjustments are needed for future mediation tasks, perhaps providing more structured templates for simplification or specific practice with turn-taking and encouragement phrases for facilitating discussions?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Nutrition" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, possibly incorporating elements of health and well-being, building on communication and explanation skills developed in this mediation lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>